# dtmf-digit-hash.hex — the DTMF digit '#' as this client transmits it,
# immediately after `dtmf-digit-5.hex` on the same call.
#
# Hand-built octet by octet from RFC 5456 (provenance rule 1,
# Tests/FIXTURES.md). Direction: us (0x0001) → peer (0x0042).
#
#   80 01        F = 1 + source call number 0x0001                     §8.1.1
#   00 42        R = 0 + destination call number 0x0042                §8.1.1
#   00 00 07 d0  32-bit time-stamp 0x7D0 = 2000 ms                     §8.1.1
#   02           OSeqno = 2. A DTMF frame is a media message carried in
#                a Full Frame, so it is reliable and "each reliable
#                message that is sent increments the message count by
#                one" — the previous digit was OSeqno 1                §7
#   02           ISeqno = 2, unchanged: nothing has been received in
#                between                                               §8.1.1
#   01           Frame Type 0x01 = DTMF                          §8.2, §8.2.1
#   23           C = 0, subclass field 0x23 = ASCII '#'. Note 0x23 is
#                also the value of IAX subclass 0x23 (Reserved, §8.4) —
#                a subclass octet means nothing without its frame type
#                                                               §8.1.1, §8.2.1
#
# Two digits, two frames, and nothing else: the RFC has no begin/end
# semantics to send in between (notes §14).
#
8001 0042 000007d0 02 02 01 23
